    "question": "What is the Hall–Petch yield-stress relation?",
    "reference": "Yield stress grows with decreasing ferrite grain size: σy = σ0 + ky·d^(-1/2), where σ0 is the internal stress and d is the grain size.",
    "answer": "The Hall–Petch yield-stress relation is given by the equation: $$ \begin{array}{c} \text{\textsigma_{y}} = \text{\textsigma_{o}} + k_{y} d^{-1/2} \\ = (\text{\textsigma_{lh}} + \text{\textsigma_{sh}} + \text{\textsigma_{dh}} + \text{\textsigma_{ph}}) + k_{y} d^{-1/2}, \text{where} \text{\textsigma_{o}} \text{is internal stress,} d \text{is grain size, and} k_{y} \text{is a constant.} \text{\textsigma_{o}} \text{is divided into several terms: lattice hardening, solid-solution hardening, dislocation hardening, and precipitation hardening.} \tag{8} \text{The equation describes how yield stress increases with decreasing grain size.} \text{Additional terms can be added to account for texture hardening and subgrain hardening.} \text{The effective grain size} d_{e} \text{can also be used in the equation.} \text{In the case of a mixed grain structure, the contribution of cells to yield strength can be accounted for by volume fractions of grain and cell.} \tag{11} \text{The equation is used to describe the strengthening mechanism in controlled-rolled steel.} \text{It is a fundamental relation in material science for predicting the yield stress of polycrystalline solids.} \text{The relation is crucial for understanding the mechanical properties of steel and other materials.} \text{The equation is often modified to include additional hardening mechanisms observed in specific microstructures.}",
    "verdict": "correct",
